---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const project = {
  title: "HR Estimation with PPG & Accelerometer",
  tag: "Biomed",
};
---

<Layout title={project.title}>
  <a href={base} class="back">← Back to projects</a>
  <article>
    <span class="tag">{project.tag}</span>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>

    <p class="summary">
      Every major wearable uses a PPG sensor to estimate heart
      rate continuously. A major challenge is that wrist movement during exercise corrupts the
      signal, often more severely than the cardiac signal itself. This project builds a
      signal processing pipeline to recover accurate heart rate from raw sensor data, using
      the accelerometer to identify and suppress movement artifacts in the frequency domain.
      Evaluated on 20 activity phases from the mHealth dataset used for the course; achieves a median
      absolute error of 4.6 BPM.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>The challenge</h2>
      <p>
        A PPG sensor works by measuring how much light the skin reflects back. As blood volume
        changes with each heartbeat, the reflected light oscillates at the cardiac frequency.
        During exercise arm movement creates a much larger oscillation in
        the same signal making it harder to detect the heartbeat. A simple frequency estimator sees the step cadence (typically 140–180
        steps/min) as the strongest periodic signal in the data and reports that as heart rate
        instead of the true value, which might be 20–40 BPM lower. The result is a sensor that
        appears to work but is silently wrong precisely when accurate HR matters most.
      </p>
      <p>
        The dataset consists of 32 recording phases (each 10–30 minutes, sampled at 128 Hz)
        from a wrist-worn device. Each phase captures a raw PPG channel and a 3-axis
        accelerometer. Ground truth HR labels are averaged over non-overlapping 30-second windows
        and are available for 20 training phases; 12 are held out. The goal is to predict HR per
        window to within as few BPM as possible, scored as the average of mean and median
        absolute error.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Signal pre-processing</h2>
      <p>
        The raw PPG is first bandpass-filtered at 128 Hz with a zero-phase Butterworth filter
        because sensor's 19-bit ADC accumulates
        slow drift from breathing and contact pressure that, if left in, shifts the dominant
        spectral peak by several BPM after decimation. Filtering first removes that drift at
        full resolution. The clean signal is then downsampled eightfold to 16 Hz which is fast enough
        to represent heart rates up to 240 BPM, but slow enough to make the subsequent FFT
        computationally cheap. The accelerometer magnitude (Euclidean norm of all three axes)
        follows the same pipeline.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}figures/hr-estimation-ppg-accel/ppg-preprocessing.png`} alt="Three-panel plot showing raw PPG at 128 Hz, bandpass-filtered PPG at 128 Hz, and downsampled signal at 16 Hz" />
        <figcaption>Top: raw 19-bit PPG with visible low-frequency baseline drift. Middle: after zero-phase Butterworth bandpass (0.55–3.5 Hz) — the cardiac oscillation is now the dominant feature. Bottom: downsampled to 16 Hz and linearly detrended, ready for spectral analysis.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Identifying motion artifacts with the accelerometer</h2>
      <p>
        The accelerometer gives a direct readout of movement. Its power spectrum has a clear
        dominant peak at the step cadence, which is the same frequency that contaminates the
        PPG. The approach is to use that peak as a guide: wherever the accelerometer shows
        strong periodic motion, reduce the weight of the PPG spectrum at that frequency rather
        than trust it blindly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Concretely, each frequency bin in the PPG spectrum is multiplied by a suppression
        factor derived from the normalised accelerometer power at that frequency. Bins where
        the accelerometer is quiet are left untouched; bins where it is energetic are
        attenuated. This soft weighting, taken from SpaMA (Salehizadeh &amp; Dao, 2016), is
        more robust than a binary on/off mask, which would create hard spectral holes that
        can swallow legitimate cardiac peaks sitting just adjacent to a cadence harmonic.
        The suppression uses all four accelerometer channels — three axes plus magnitude — and
        takes the maximum across them, so movement that appears only on one axis (wrist
        pronation, for instance) is still caught.
      </p>
      <p>
        An additional hard exclusion addresses a failure mode that the soft suppression alone
        missed. During running at cadences above 120 steps/min, the slow lateral arm swing
        produces a secondary oscillation at exactly one-third of the step frequency (50–60 BPM).
        Since this falls inside the cardiac frequency band, it was initially
        indistinguishable from a genuine low heart rate. The bug surfaced by plotting the
        spectra of sub-windows where the algorithm was reporting 55 BPM against a ground truth
        of 140 BPM: a small but clean peak at f<sub>step</sub>/3 was being selected as the
        cardiac frequency. Adding an explicit exclusion zone around that sub-harmonic during
        high-cadence running fixed the class of errors entirely.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}figures/hr-estimation-ppg-accel/spama-spectra.png`} alt="PPG and ACC power spectra for a 6-second sub-window, showing candidate HR peaks and the motion artifact exclusion zone" />
        <figcaption>PPG spectrum (left) and ACC magnitude spectrum (right) for a representative 6-second sub-window. Three candidate HR peaks are detected at 53, 85, and 112 BPM (green dashed lines); the ground truth label is 87 BPM (black dotted). The dominant ACC peak at 117 BPM (pink shading) identifies the step cadence — well above the cardiac band here, so suppression selects the wrong peak at 53 BPM. This class of error motivated adding the temporal continuity prior, which would penalise a jump from a prior estimate near 87 BPM to 53 BPM.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Tracking HR over time</h2>
      <p>
        Even after artifact suppression, the PPG spectrum sometimes contains multiple plausible
        peaks. To choose between them, each candidate is scored by combining its spectral power
        with how far it sits from the previous estimate. A large jump in HR from one 6-second
        window to the next is physiologically unlikely, so candidates requiring a large jump
        are penalised. This continuity prior is weighted at 40% of the total score, leaving
        the majority of the decision to the spectral evidence. If all candidates are flagged as
        artifacts, the previous estimate is held constant; any gaps that accumulate are filled
        by cubic-spline interpolation across clean surrounding windows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Aggregating over sub-windows</h2>
      <p>
        Rather than processing each 30-second label window as a single block, the pipeline
        splits it into five consecutive 6-second sub-windows and runs the full estimation on
        each. The five resulting estimates are then combined by median. The reason to prefer
        median over mean is robustness to corruption from the noise.
        A 3-point median filter across successive 30-second outputs
        provides a final smoothing pass without introducing the lag of a causal filter.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}figures/hr-estimation-ppg-accel/ppg-subwindow-tracking.png`} alt="Timeline plot showing 6-second sub-window HR estimates as scattered dots, their per-block median as a blue line, and ground truth labels as orange crosses" />
        <figcaption>Individual 6-second sub-window estimates (blue dots) and their per-block median (blue line) plotted against ground truth labels (orange). Outlier sub-windows — where suppression fails on a particularly noisy burst — are isolated and do not pull the block median off course.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Tuning and results</h2>
      <p>The pipeline has four tunable parameters, optimised via grid search over 180 combinations on the training phases:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cadence exclusion zone width</strong>: narrowing from 0.02 Hz to 0.01 Hz improved performance; the wider zone was clipping legitimate cardiac peaks near low-power accelerometer frequencies.</li>
        <li><strong>Sub-harmonic exclusion zone width</strong>: controls the region suppressed around f<sub>step</sub>/3 during high-cadence running.</li>
        <li><strong>Maximum HR jump between sub-windows</strong>: caps the continuity penalty, preventing over-smoothing during genuine rapid HR changes.</li>
        <li><strong>Median filter kernel size</strong>: governs the final smoothing pass across successive 30-second block estimates.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        With tuned parameters, the pipeline achieves a median absolute error of 4.6 BPM and a
        mean absolute error of 11.7 BPM (combined score 8.2 BPM). The gap between the two
        metrics is informative: the median tells us that more than half of all 30-second windows
        are estimated within 4.6 BPM of truth, which is competitive with commercial wearables
        during moderate activity. The higher mean is driven by a small number of windows in
        high-motion phases where the step cadence and true cardiac frequency are too close to
        separate spectrally, a remaining hard problem for any frequency-domain approach.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src={`${base}figures/hr-estimation-ppg-accel/spama-error-dist.png`} alt="Error distribution histogram and predicted vs ground truth scatter plot on the training set" />
        <figcaption>Left: absolute error distribution across all training windows. The majority of estimates fall within 5 BPM of ground truth (median 4.5 BPM), but a long tail extends to 60+ BPM — concentrated in high-motion phases where cadence and cardiac frequency overlap. Right: predicted vs ground truth scatter. Most points track the identity line closely; the cluster of points below the line at higher ground-truth HR values corresponds to windows where the algorithm locks onto the step cadence (~60 BPM) instead of the true cardiac frequency.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
      <p>
        The remaining errors are concentrated in windows where the runner's heart rate happens
        to match their step rate which is a genuinely ambiguous situation for any spectral method. Two
        directions would address this. First, using the per-axis accelerometer spectra rather
        than just the magnitude would give finer control over axis-specific artifacts, since
        different movement patterns load different axes. Second, a small
        recurrent network trained on (PPG, ACC) pairs could learn to exploit temporal context
        and cross-channel correlations that a hand-crafted spectral filter cannot. The 20
        training phases (~480 minutes of labelled data) would be a reasonable starting point,
        though more diversity in activity type would strengthen generalisation.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-decoration: none; color: #555; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .back:hover { color: #1a1a1a; }
  .tag { font-size: 0.72rem; background: #f0f4ff; color: #3b5bdb; padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 999px; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 650; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; color: #1a1a1a; }
  .summary { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #333; max-width: 680px; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  section { max-width: 680px; }
  p { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #333; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  code { font-family: 'Menlo', 'Monaco', monospace; font-size: 0.88em; background: #f5f5f5; padding: 0.1em 0.35em; border-radius: 4px; }
  ul { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #333; margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem 1.25rem; padding: 0; }
  ul li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  figure { margin: 1.5rem 0; max-width: 100%; background: #f8f9fa; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; }
  figure img { width: 100%; max-width: 900px; height: auto; display: block; }
  figure .placeholder {
    background: #f0f1f5;
    border-bottom: 1px dashed #bcc0d0;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: #888;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.5;
    min-height: 120px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
  }
  figcaption { font-size: 0.82rem; color: #666; line-height: 1.5; padding: 0.6rem 1rem; border-top: 1px solid #e5e7eb; }
</style>
